# Exam track — Theme: Describing a Picture — Sub-lesson 2: Person Description
#
# The WHO task for the funfair photo of sub-lesson 1: who is present, and 1–2 of
# them described visually. The examiner's second prompt on the picture task is
# almost always "pick one person and describe them" — hair, clothes, what they
# are doing — so this drills exactly that.
#
# The photo detail this describes: a young woman with dark, shoulder-length hair
# in a black top and blue denim shorts, carrying a black-and-white patterned
# handbag; next to her a boy in a green top and light shorts, smiling, holding
# her hand.
#
# PURE DESCRIPTION, same as 01 — no @question prompts, no first-person opinion.
# A depicted person's visible emotion (`De Jong lächelt`) is observation and is
# wanted; what the *learner* feels about them is not.
#
# Same A1–A2 constraint as sub-lesson 1, with one extra guard that appearance
# descriptions make easy to break: attributive adjectives are used ONLY on
# feminine nouns (`eng blo Box`, `eng hell Box`), where Luxembourgish leaves the
# adjective uninflected. Masculine and neuter attributives (`en schwaarzen
# T-Shirt`, `e frëndlecht Gesiicht`) need declension the learner hasn't met, so
# colours are stated predicatively instead (`D'Posch ass schwaarz a wäiss.`).
#
# Words sub-lesson 1 already teaches (d'Fra, de Jong, d'Leit, trëppelen, frou, …)
# are deliberately NOT repeated here — the theme's pass-gate makes 01 a
# prerequisite for 02, and stat keys are shared across the app. Colours and
# clothes live here rather than in 01, because appearance is this file's task.
#
# The three @fill blocks carry the frames that recur in every person
# description: the relative clause `eng Fra, déi … huet` (which the learner
# reads rather than assembles — verb-final, so it belongs in a frame, not a
# @sentence), the possessive hair frame, and the `hunn … un` clothing frame.
# The comma in the relative-clause frame also stops the Eifeler Regel, which is
# what makes a blank safe right after it. See references/content-contract.md.

@lesson P1.02 "Schueberfouer: Person Description"

# Same photo as 01, declared again: @image is per-file, and the learner must see
# the photo during THIS sub-lesson too. The alt text differs on purpose — it
# describes the detail this file drills.
@image "/assets/exam/picture/schueberfouer/img/schueberfouer.webp"
@image-alt "The same funfair photo, seen for the people in it: a young woman with dark shoulder-length hair in a black top and blue denim shorts, a black-and-white handbag over her shoulder, holding the hand of a smiling boy in a green top and light shorts."

# --- Hair ---
@word d'Hoer = the hair
@word donkel = dark
@word hell = light
@word laang = long
@word kuerz = short
@word brong = brown

# --- Clothes ---
@word droen = to wear
@word den T-Shirt = the T-shirt
@word d'Box = the shorts
@word d'Posch = the handbag
@word de Brëll = the glasses

# --- Colours ---
@word blo = blue
@word gréng = green
@word schwaarz = black
@word wäiss = white

# --- People ---
@word d'Mamm = the mother
@word d'Kanner = the children
@word d'Hand = the hand
@word jonk = young

# --- Doing things ---
@word halen = to hold
@word lächelen = to smile
@word nieft = next to
@word zesummen = together

@sentence
@lu D'Fra ass jonk an hir Hoer sinn donkel.
@en The woman is young and her hair is dark.
@distractor-lu al
@distractor-lu hell
@distractor-en old
@distractor-en light

@sentence
@lu Si dréit eng blo Box an en T-Shirt.
@en She is wearing blue shorts and a T-shirt.
@distractor-lu hell
@distractor-lu Posch
@distractor-en light
@distractor-en handbag

@sentence
@lu Hiren T-Shirt ass schwaarz.
@en Her T-shirt is black.
@distractor-lu gréng
@distractor-lu wäiss
@distractor-en green
@distractor-en white

@sentence
@lu D'Posch ass schwaarz a wäiss.
@en The handbag is black and white.
@distractor-lu blo
@distractor-lu gréng
@distractor-en blue
@distractor-en green

@sentence
@lu Si hält d'Hand vum Jong.
@en She is holding the boy's hand.
@distractor-lu kuckt
@distractor-lu Posch
@distractor-en looks
@distractor-en handbag

@sentence
@lu Si ass d'Mamm vum Jong.
@en She is the boy's mother.
@distractor-lu Meedchen
@distractor-lu Fra
@distractor-en girl
@distractor-en woman

@sentence
@lu De Jong trëppelt nieft der Fra a lächelt.
@en The boy is walking next to the woman and smiling.
@distractor-lu hannert
@distractor-lu steet
@distractor-en behind
@distractor-en standing

@sentence
@lu De Jong dréit eng hell Box an den T-Shirt ass gréng.
@en The boy is wearing light shorts and the T-shirt is green.
@distractor-lu donkel
@distractor-lu schwaarz
@distractor-en dark
@distractor-en black

@sentence
@lu Si trëppelen zesummen op der Fouer.
@en They are walking together at the fair.
@distractor-lu eleng
@distractor-lu stinn
@distractor-en alone
@distractor-en standing

# --- Reusable frames (@fill) ---

# The relative-clause frame. `déi … huet` is verb-final, so the learner reads
# the order instead of assembling it — precisely the split between @fill and
# @sentence. The comma also ends the clause, which is what makes the blank after
# `Fra` safe under the Eifeler Regel. Numeral in the frame, noun in the blank:
# `zwee` forces a plural, so singular `Kand` and `Meedchen` are both wrong.
@fill
@lu Ech gesinn eng Fra, déi zwee [Kanner] huet.
@en I see a woman who has two [children].
@distractor-lu Kand
@distractor-lu Meedchen
@distractor-en child
@distractor-en girl

# The possessive hair frame — `Hir Hoer si …` recurs for any person.
#
# ONE blank on purpose. The two-blank version (`si [laang] a [brong]`) looks
# fine and is an R6 violation: both blanks are predicative adjectives joined by
# `a`, with no forced order, so `brong a laang` is equally true and grammatical
# — a second correct answer the grader would mark wrong. Blanking one side is
# the fix (see R6 in the SKILL).
@fill
@lu Hir Hoer si laang a [brong].
@en Her hair is long and [brown].
@distractor-lu blo
@distractor-lu gréng
@distractor-en blue
@distractor-en green

# The `hunn … un` clothing frame — the separable `un` sits at the end, another
# order the learner should read rather than build. `e Brëll` is masculine with
# the determiner fixed in the frame, so feminine `Posch` and `Box` are ruled out
# on grammar alone (R4).
@fill
@lu Si huet e [Brëll] un.
@en She is wearing [glasses].
@distractor-lu Posch
@distractor-lu Box
@distractor-en a handbag
@distractor-en shorts
